  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "Members of an armed group, including child soldiers, are protected persons under Article 8(2)(e)(vi) of the Rome Statute and may therefore be victims of the war crimes of rape and sexual slavery even when the crimes are committed by fellow members of the same armed group.",
      "reasoning": "The definition of \"protected persons\" in the Rome Statute and the Geneva Conventions is based on the person's status in the conflict, not on the identity of the perpetrator. International humanitarian law prohibits sexual violence against any person who is not taking a direct part in hostilities, which includes members of the same armed group who are hors de combat, detained, or otherwise vulnerable. Consequently, the elements of the crime are satisfied when the act of rape or sexual slavery is committed in the context of a non‑international armed conflict, regardless of the perpetrator’s affiliation.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The jurisdiction of the ICC under Article 8(2)(e)(vi) is triggered by the commission of the prohibited act in the context of an armed conflict, not by the relationship between victim and perpetrator.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(e)(vi) criminalises \"rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, forced pregnancy, enforced sterilisation or any other form of sexual violence\" when committed in a non‑international armed conflict. The statutory elements focus on the act, the context, and the victim’s protected status, not on whether the perpetrator belongs to the same armed group. Therefore, the Court’s jurisdiction is proper where the factual elements are met, irrespective of intra‑group dynamics.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Customary international humanitarian law, as reflected in the jurisprudence of international tribunals, affirms that war crimes may be committed against members of the same armed group.",
      "reasoning": "Customary IHL treats the prohibition of sexual violence as a universal norm that applies to all parties in a conflict. International tribunals (e.g., the ICTY and ICC) have held that the victim’s status as a member of an armed group does not immunise the perpetrator when the victim is a protected person. This principle supports the view that intra‑group sexual violence falls within the scope of war crimes.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The defence’s contention that protection only arises after a combatant ceases to be a member of the armed group is inconsistent with the established interpretation of \"protected persons\" in IHL.",
      "reasoning": "Protected persons include those who are \"out of combat\" due to detention, injury, or other circumstances, even while still formally belonging to the armed group. International law recognises that child soldiers and other vulnerable members retain protected status precisely because they are often unable to voluntarily cease participation. Hence, the defence’s argument is contrary to the protective purpose of the statutes.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Even in the absence of specific treaty citations, the principle of individual criminal responsibility under the Rome Statute requires that the perpetrator be held accountable for the prohibited act, regardless of the victim’s group affiliation.",
      "reasoning": "The Rome Statute’s framework of individual criminal responsibility focuses on the commission of the prohibited conduct and the requisite mental element. The victim’s identity as a fellow member of the armed group does not negate the existence of the crime, nor does it preclude the Court’s jurisdiction. This principle reinforces the legitimacy of prosecuting intra‑group sexual violence as war crimes.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
